\f4\fs26 A lone Sarah arrived a few minutes early at the Memphis Mafia Bar and was met at the reception desk by an Elvi-girl named Elvine. As one might expect, she was the spitting image of the waitress from the night before, but knowing she was one of many, many clones in the quarter did not diminish the wonder of precise human replicas at large. Their faculties were exactly the same, displaying the self-same mannerisms, affectations and gait.\
\'93Good afternoon. My name is Elvine and I will be your hostess here at the Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill today. Will you be dining or would you like to take a place at the bar?\'94\
\'93Thank you, Elvine. Do you think I would be able to get a private room?\'94 She asked, most politely.\
\'93Of course, but it will cost you.\'94 Elvine replied, smiling widely. \'93Girls will cost you more again, but you know that don\'92t you, honey?\'94\
\'93Just the room will be fine, thank you Elvine.\'94 Sarah was very glad she decided to leave Cracker at home. Apart from his lumpy, scorched head drawing attention, she had just avoided the hassle of shutting him down when he took up the offer of prostitutes. \'93I am expecting a friend, shortly.\'94\
\'93Just follow me.\'94 Even in the early afternoon, the Memphis Mafia bar was full of drunks and drug-fucked scum from the quarter and as they snaked their way to the rear, it was clear that most patrons were far more interested in drinking than eating. Not one table had any evidence of a meal having been consumed, not a packet of crisps nor a stray salt crystal. The \'93private room\'94 was nothing more than a grandiose booth, separated from the rest of the bar with sleazy burgundy velvet drapes. \'93Here you are, the finest in the house.\'94 Elvine proudly announced as she drew back the ugly curtains with gaudy faux-gold ropes to reveal large U-shaped booth with red leather upholstery and an ornately carved wooden table that managed to gleam, notwithstanding the dim light. \
 \
\'93Wow is that leather? I mean authentic leather?\'94 Sarah faux-gushed, pretending to be genuinely impressed.\
\'93Sure is sweetie. It will even squeak when you sit.\'94 Replied a smiling Elvine.\
She wasted no time in being seated, gleefully fidgeting to maximise the squeaks while Elvine rolled her eyes.\
\'93Can I help with any drinks?\'94\
\'93Thank you Elvine, but no. Not at this stage. I think I will wait for my friend.\'94 Sarah said, politely as possible.\
Elvine headed for the bar and Sarah immediately pulled out her palm device, made a quick entry and then began to point it randomly around the room. She then made a beeline to the far corner, patting down the drapes and locating an ancient microphone and transmitter. She then spun and headed to the opposite corner where she was able to locate a small, but equally elderly camera. When she was satisfied the room was clean she squeaked back into the booth. \
Sarah fidgeted, trying to garner some comfort from the cheap, red sinewy cardboard assailing her backside. John Finagle, known to Sarah as Johnny, entered the booth with a look of horror and disgust. Never having been on street level he could not get over the stench or the countenances of the butt-ugly inhabitants of this alternate universe. He was, naturally, totally over-dressed for such a sordid venue \'96 the black leather shoes (quality leather) gleamed; artfully understated dark blue trousers sported knife edge creases and his choice of shirt, tie and jacket complimented and contrasted. \
\'93Did you get a look at some of the heads out there? It looks like a leper\'92s grog den. Haven\'92t they heard of cosmetic surgery? And what is that smell? My God, I am going to have to burn these clothes when I get home.\'94 Johnny was not happy unless he had something to complain about and he was in full whinge-mode. \'93What is this stuff stuck to my shoes?\'94\
\'93That would be mud.\'94\
\'93This is some crappy dive, Sarah. What is so important that you have to drag me here?\'94 Johnny inquired as he took up a seat in the booth, deliberately sitting close, too close to Sarah. She calmly slid away. \
\'93Have a look at this.\'94 Sarah offered a copper mesh container, under a napkin, to Johnny.\
 \
\'93A Faraday-mini! What\'92s in here then?\'94 Johnny opened the small cage and his eyes immediately widened and as he examined it, a look of pure amazement took over his face. \'93Holy tax audit! Where did you get this?\'94 \
\'93I found it.\'94\
\'93That does not sound likely, my friend. You don\'92t just find one of these.\'94 Johnny said, punctuating each word with incredulity. \
\'93It was left in my office after the death of my predecessor. What do you think it is, Johnny?\'94 asked Sarah, transparently deflecting his question.\
\'93I have never seen one, only heard of them. It\'92s a Gyges Disc. They provide a cloak of invisibility to all electronic surveillance. Cameras, microphones don\'92t see or hear you. It\'92s a hall-pass for every building in the Primary Trade Dominion \'96 you could really get yourself in trouble with one of these, Sarah.\'94\
\'93Yes, discretion is everything.\'94 She threatened. \
\'93If that thing is working, there was no need to drag me to this disgusting excuse for a bar, all surveillance in a fifty metre radius will be out of action.\'94 \
\'93What about the cage?\'94\
 \
\'93A Faraday cage will do no good, I\'92ve heard they pick up on gravity waves. The only way I can think to render it inert is to spin it, very fast. The acceleration at the out edge should overcome the influence of gravity waves.\'94 He projected confidently.\
\'93Do they cancel each other out?\'94 Sarah inquired. \
\'93Something like that.\'94 He really had no idea how the disc operated, but hubris insisted he puff out his chest and display full plumage. \'93My pension fund runneth over! Look, I think it has bio-components and if I am not mistaken this is a bio-quantum processor \'96 I have to get this back to my lab.\'94 \
 \
Sarah did not fancy the idea of either Johnny or Cracker with the keys to the kingdom in hand. No decent girl would be safe in her bed! As an adolescent she had been schooled in the world of men, exposed to the awful truth when successfully deflecting her very first date-rape. Since that sobering revelation, she had witnessed precious little to overturn the view that 
\f5\i \'93men are just selfish oafs groping, slobbering, farting and ejaculating at will\'94
\f4\i0  leaving a swathe of destruction in their paths. She and the rest of female kind were eternally dumbfounded that males are ignorant of their own malevolence or, worse still, men are aware of it and it simply does not bother them. Johnny, she had to admit, had never given her reason to distrust, but she figured, it was just a matter of time before his true colours emerged.\
\'93No. I don\'92t think this should be in the hands of a man, any man.\'94 She said with a hint of sarcasm. \
\'93Overt sexism from you? I never would have thought, Sarah. Have I ever behaved in any manner other than a gentleman?\'94 Johnny waited for Sarah to answer, but found himself answering for her, \'93No, No I haven\'92t.\'94\
\'93It\'92s not sexism if a woman says it.\'94 She quoted the widely acknowledged truth. 
\f5\i Doesn\'92t he know that? 
\f4\i0 \'93What are you going to do with it? What tests?\'94 \
\'93I don\'92t know exactly, but the first thing is to get this device spinning. I don\'92t suppose you have a centrifuge on you?\'94 Johnny let out a small, self-conscious laugh, hoping Sarah might too. \
\'93No, no centrifuge.\'94 She forced a vague smile, but there was no laugh. 
\f5\i Cracker is funnier.
\f4\i0 \
\'93No problems.\'94 He poked his head out of the kitsch curtain configuration and managed to get the attention of Elvine. \
\'93Be polite.\'94 Sarah warned.\
\'93To an L.1 waitress? You are kidding of course.\'94 He said with the confidence of the uninitiated. \
\'93Yes, darlin\'92 how can I help you?\'94 Elvine asked courteously.\
\'93Give me a small cordless stick blender or food processor. Now.\'94 Johnny demanded.\
\'93This is a bar and restaurant, sir, not an appliance store.\'94 Elvine retorted.\
\'93There will be one behind the bar. Go get it.\'94 He ordered, not even making eye-contact.\
\'93Yes, sir.\'94 She spun around and headed in the direction of the bar.\
\'93She took that pretty well. I was told they are not fond of the impolite.\'94 Sarah was most surprised. The way Cracker had explained it to her, you would find yourself in a very dangerous place if you are discourteous to the Elvi.\
\'93She knows how to take orders from her superiors.\'94 He smiled back. There was no way he would suck-up to a fucking beverage delivery system, his 
\f5\i dignitas 
\f4\i0 demanded otherwise.\
Elvine returned in double quick time, carrying a old glass blender. The glass \'91jug\'92 was in her right hand and the small base in her left. \
\'93I will just need the base. You know, the motor.\'94 Johnny said, sticking his hand out, again no eye contact.\
\'93You will get both.\'94 Elvine brought down the jug onto Johnny\'92s head, the glass shattering and slicing into his arrogant baldness. Blood raced out across his skull, then in-between a mash of interwoven fingers that failed to stem the flow. He collapsed and without a sound, he curled up in a ball on the sticky red carpet.\
\'93You will not leave until you pay for the damage and the cleaning bill.\'94 She placed a small slip of poly-paper on the table, whirled around and paced angrily to the bar, this time chatting sternly to a security guard who immediately headed in their direction. Sarah snatched the disc, depositing it inside her brassiere, the metal warming her supple skin. She then considered Johnny who was now motionless, barely conscious and haemorrhaging badly. She would defend him from further damage, his poor behaviour notwithstanding. \
She would be ready when the bouncer arrived and he would be in for the shock of his life, however, he seemed to take an age to arrive. She poked her head out again and saw that a tall, broad shouldered young man, face concealed by a black hooded jacket, was in an animated discussion with the absentee bouncer. She was unable to glean any of the conversation but arms flapped about, fingers poked and wagged, middle digits were unleashed, but shortly they appeared to arrive at some accommodation. The two parted ways, the black hood headed towards the private booths and presented himself before Sarah, revealing a burnt and swollen face.\
\'93Cracker!\'94 She laughed, noting that, in identifying himself, he suddenly seemed shorter and generally less impressive. \'93What are you doing here?\'94\
\'93Saving your behind, again.\'94 
\f5\i That\'92s twice. She\'92s gotta at least give me a hand-crank now.
\f4\i0 \
\'93Thank the laws of economics you are here. What did you tell him?\'94\
\'93That you would pay double the bill.\'94 He low-balled the figure to see how she reacted.\
\'93Double? That seems excessive.\'94 She didn\'92t really care, just as long as they got out of there without any further trouble. She crouched next to Johnny and firmly placed a cushion over the gash in his crown and lashed it down using his stylish tie as a chin strap. 
\f5\i Double the bill?
\f4\i0 \
\'93Well they did not see it that way at all. So we ended up settling on three times the bill.\'94 He decided to come clean.\
\'93Remind me never to let you negotiate for me again.\'94 She was almost smiling, but lugging Johnny-boy out was proving to be difficult, but Cracker pitched in and they slowly made the journey to the exit, settling the bill on the way. \'93Could we not have just paid 100%?\'94 Sarah seemed a little puzzled.\
\'93Yes, but that comes with a complimentary beating.\'94 Cracker informed her. \'93They near kill you.\'94\
\'93Not me\'94 Sarah said with some degree of confidence. \'93I might have been able to give them a surprise.\'94\
\'93Yes, but that would have drawn some unwanted attention as well.\'94 \
\'93Maybe a little.\'94 She giggled with pride. She could not imagine living in fear, enduring a pathetic, cowering, shadow of an existence, like the many wasted lives feminine kind had suffered through the ages. She had many times pondered what the crushing oppression would have done to her. What sort of a person would she have become? She figured women of her ilk were especially hated and hunted down as witches. 
\f5\i Yes, I would have been burned alive.
\f4\i0 \
\'93I think you would have attracted way too much attention if you cleaned up that bouncer. He was fucking huge.\'94 He was not kidding.\
 \
\'93You are right of course, but at least we have the disc. We should not appear on any security logs anywhere along the way.\'94\
\'93Where to now?\'94 Cracker asked.\
\'93\cb2 It\'92ll have to be your apartment.\cb1  We can\'92t take him to a Medical Centre around here and we won\'92t be able to get anywhere near the Management District without questions being asked. No-one else can be present when he wakes, there\'92s no telling what he will say. Chances are he won\'92t be too discreet, especially when he\'92s in pain.\'94 Johnny was not one to bare discomfort in silence. He could scarcely handle a pin prick without moaning about it for weeks. His current soul destroying malady came in the form of a mild case of contact dermatitis, but you could be forgiven for thinking he was suffering from necrotic chancres on his ball bag, such were the unending complaints. \
Dragging the semi-responsive, bleeding carcass of Johnny was hard work and with no taxis willing to take a half-dead customer, they had \cb2 no choice but to chance the travelator \cb1 across town and through The Pale. The implementation of the travelator, a closed circle of small box cars connected front to tail on a prehistoric tram track, was greeted by many as a great step in servicing the transport needs of the lows. The 37 kilometre ring of train carriages perpetually circumnavigated the city at brisk walking pace and for a small fee anyone could jump on and off at any time as it meandered through some of the shittiest sites in the all the dominion. \
\cb2 The Travelator, one of the last investments in public transport infrastructure, however, proved to be an archetypal demonstration of chaos theory in action - the best laid plans of men immediately turning to shit.\cb1  Directly upon the first wheel hitting the track, homeless families, criminal gangs and opportunists of every variety moved in, taking over many of the cars and fiercely defending their territories. Carriages became wandering brothels, drug dens and mobile homes for the lucky homeless who managed to stake a claim. Subsequently, for those idiotic enough to make the attempt, the cost of a ride was dependent entirely on which car you jumped up upon. Cracker had never taken a ride but, the word among his contemporaries was to address the 
\f5\i Travelatorisi
\f4\i0  as you would a king and they may only seek to fleece you. He knew with certainty, however, that some would pretty much kill you no matter what. At a glance there was no way of telling which car was the right one for you, but as each slowly clicked by, an obvious scent of rotting flesh and a swarm of blow-flies was a rank clue to give that one a miss. 
\f5\i Travelatorisi
\f4\i0  stood at entrance ramps touting for business with promises of a cheap ride, your safety their primary concern, while others attracted fresh prey with gaudy colours, flashing lights and loud music. Sometimes they snatched-up hapless pedestrians that had strayed too close to the tracks, demand payment for the ride before push them off, occasionally still alive.\
\'93We have to just pick one and hope for the best. Only the desperate and the stupid take this ride and at this point I think we qualify as both.\'94 Cracker told Sarah as they approached the track. \'93You get on first and then pull him up, as I push.\'94\
Sarah seamlessly sashayed from the grimy cement onto the entrance ramp and began dragging Johnny up by his coat, Cracker pushing at his flabby backside with sufficient force to knock Sarah on her back and throw Johnny on top. \
\'93Geez, smooth operator, isn\'92t he?\'94 Cracker laughed as Sarah pushed and squirmed to extricate herself from under the dead weight. Rather than being met by raised voices and gun muzzles, they were relieved the only other occupants, three men wearing hooded cloaks drawn in at the waist with cord, were seated quietly in the corner and barely seemed to notice their arrival. Cracker rightly assumed they were the resident 
\f5\i travelatorisi
\f4\i0 , but most atypical. Silent still, the elder of the three slowly raised his eyes and momentarily reflected upon the trio before returning his gaze to his feet, hood concealing his face.\
\cb2 \'93Travelatorisi, we seek safe passage through the Pale and wish to pay tribute.\'94 Cracker gingerly commenced. \cb1 \
\'93In the name of science and reason, who are you to receive such munificence?\'94 Inquired the elder calmly, serenely. \
\'93I am Cracker of the Hardy clan, wise one. I am escorting my friends to safety, but as you see, one is wounded, I think badly, great Travelatorisi.\'94 He thought he might be laying it on a little thick, but it seemed to be working.\
\'93You are most humble come forward and let me have a look at you. What has entropy brought to me? A pretty girl and two crash victims?\'94 said the elder. \'93Tell me traveller, do you flee justice or do you seek it?\'94\
\'93We seek justice.\'94 Cracker replied, hoping it was the right answer.\
\'93Then you shall have safe passage. The protection of The Dawkins shall be yours\'94 He stood up and hugged Cracker lightly and then Sarah, tightly, kissing her on both cheeks. \'93I am Kleobou and these are my apprentices, Silo and Chilo.\'94 They both nodded, but remained silent. \'93It seems this one is in need of assistance.\'94 He glanced at his two apprentices and immediately they were kneeling either side of Johnny, gently removing the cushion from his head. \
\'93Silo and Chilo will clean his wounds and we shall talk.\'94 Kleobou smiled widely, exposing a set of stained teeth and motioned them to a cluster of wooden chairs and a small table in the corner, one small bulb swinging above. They sat and Kleobou offered wine which they both accepted, against their better judgment. Cracker and Sarah were offered small wooden cups, but Kleo, his preferred name he explained, gulped the red liquid from a golden bowl. Cracker followed suit and when Sarah was convinced the offering was neither drugged nor poisoned, she sipped lightly and finding herself pleasantly surprised by the taste, quaffed half the cup down. \
\'93This is a really quite an acceptable vintage. Thank you.\'94 Sarah smiled brightly, face glowing. \'93Shiraz?\'94\
\'93It seems you know grapes.\'94 Her response to the wine had confirmed what he already knew. \'93You are not of the third estate. How do you find yourself here, young miss?\'94 He gently gazed into her eyes, penetrating deeply but causing her no alarm or unease. To the contrary she felt awash with a warm tranquility. \
\'93I could claim a set of unforeseen and unfortunate circumstances, but I have only myself to blame.\'94 She confessed. She did not know why she implicitly trusted this fellow, but she felt a compulsion to unburden her soul and tell him whatever he wanted to know. Alarm bells in her head rang silent.\
\'93We are here because fuck-knuckle over there can\'92t play nice with the plebeians.\'94 Cracker insisted on pointing out. \
\'93I put Johnny in an alien situation and I should have known better. It was his first time at street level.\'94 She insisted. \'93It was never going to end well.\'94\
\'93Do not desire the impossible, Sarah. Your friend is a product of his environment, he could not have acted any differently.\'94 Kleo counseled.\
\'93But what of my upbringing? I have the same pedigree.\'94 She knew she was divulging far too much information, but it felt good. \
\'93You are different, but you already know that, don\'92t you?\'94 He spoke with such a degree of certainty that Sarah found herself compelled to listen. \'93You are unique, you will do great things if you use your intelligence, but follow your heart. Don\'92t be duped by your conditioning, your world is not as it seems, the structures holding it fast are made of chalk, not steel.\'94\
\'93Aren\'92t you supposed to be staring into a crystal ball or magic fire or something?\'94 Cracker was in no doubt that this was a load of crap. 
\f5\i What would some idiot hiding out in a train carriage know about life? This is not a Himalayan cave.
\f4\i0 \
\'93I see you are a skeptic, young man. Let me look at you. You claim membership of the great Hardy clan?\'94 Kleo said, now staring into Cracker\'92s pupils, but young Hardy did not feel warm and fuzzy, but violated. It felt like Kleo was somewhere deep in his brain, poking about and periodically interrogating neurons. \'93You are of the Hardy clan! Perhaps even a descendant of Jamison Hardy himself, if I am not mistaken\'94\
\'93Who?\'94 Cracker did not know.\
\'93The last man to try.\'94 Kleo said cryptically, a hint of sadness seeping out. \'93He rejected violence and walked the path of peace and love.\'94\
\'93What a pussy.\'94 Cracker blurted out without thinking. \
\'93He once stood as the only man that could stop that.\'94 Kleo pointed out the entrance of the carriage and into the dim daylight. \'93The Primary Trade Dominion. Jamison Hardy sought to understand his fellow man, to pick him up not stomp him into the mud. He was no pussy, young Hardy. I thought he was the one-after-the-one.\'94\
\'93The one-after-the-one.\'94 Silo and Chilo chimed in unison.\
\'93My apologies, Kleo.\'94 Cracker\'92s apology was genuine, but he still did not believe the Jamison Hardy rubbish. \'93I find it hards to accept you can tell anything, just by looking in my eyes.\'94\
\'93Your eyes are virtually identical. Colour, pattern, right down to the red fleck below the left pupil.\'94 Kleo quietly insisted.\
\'93Did you know him?\'94 Sarah was now quite intrigued.\
\'93I had the honour of being in his employ for many years. How is the patient, Silo?\'94\
\'93We have bound the wound on his head, but he is concussed. I don\'92t think there is any bleeding or swelling to the brain. His pupils are the same size and reactive to light. That is all we can do, Master. I expect he will recover in a few days.\'94 Solo replied, reverently bowing his head.\
\'93I have heard the legend of Jamison Hardy, but I thought he was an urban myth.\'94 Sarah said, drawing from distant memory. \'93If I recall correctly it was a cautionary tale. Jamison Hardy, traitor to capitalism.\'94 \
 \
\'93His memory is much maligned. He was a true man of the people, so much so he, too, was caught up in the Great Viral Cleansing and died with the masses.\'94 Kleo recalled with some obvious distress. \'93We are approaching The Pale.\'94 Silo and Chilo set to work locking doors and shutters, double checking each other\'92s work. \'93We take on no passengers in The Pale it is far too dangerous, but we will be safe enough in here. Well my new friends we should be through the worst of it in about an hour.\'94 
